    33   * specify the offset within the mesh and the meshPartSize is used to 
specify the part (in total
    34   * number of vertices) to render. If the mesh is indexed (which is when 
{@link Mesh#getNumIndices()}
    35   * > 0) then both values are in number of indices within the indices 
array of the mesh, otherwise
    36   * they are in number of vertices within the vertices array of the mesh.
Note that some classes
    37   * might require the mesh to be indexed. The {@link #material} and 
(optional) {@link #environment}
    38   * values are combined to specify how the shape should look like. 
Typically these are used to
    39   * specify uniform values or other OpenGL state changes. When a value is
present in both the {@link
    40   * #material} and {@link #environment}, then the value of the {@link 
#material} will be used.
    41   * Renderables can be rendered directly using a {@link Shader} (in which
case the {@link #shader}
    42   * member is ignored). Though more typically Renderables are rendered 
via a {@link ModelBatch},
    43   * either directly, or by passing a {@link RenderableProvider} like 
{@link ModelInstance} to the
